# Path to the JSON timeline store the API reads and writes.
#
# Point this at your own data file. It is the source of truth (see docs/data-store.md), not
# a derived artifact — nothing regenerates it, so nothing will silently overwrite your edits:
# DATA_FILE_PATH='/path/to/timeline-data.json'
#
# Or, to run against the fabricated sample data, use the copy in the build output. The
# build refreshes it every time, so writes never dirty the checked-in file:
# DATA_FILE_PATH='./bin/Debug/net10.0/test/sample-01.json'
#
# Note: dotenv.net OVERWRITES real environment variables, so exporting DATA_FILE_PATH in
# your shell will not override this file. Edit it here instead.
#
# A missing file or an unset value fails at startup with an explicit message rather than
# quietly serving an empty timeline.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Episode images (all optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The store holds only a file name; the bytes live on disk, laid out as:
#
#   <image root>/<timeline id>/<file name>
#
# By default the image root is "images" beside DATA_FILE_PATH, so a timeline is one portable
# directory — the JSON and its pictures together. Set this only if they have to live apart;
# two independently configured paths drift, and then "portable" stops being true.
# IMAGE_ROOT_PATH='/path/to/images'
#
# A missing image root is not an error. Images are optional, and an episode whose image is
# absent draws exactly like one that never had an image.
#
# Put originals in the directory and nothing else: thumbnails are generated on demand and
# cached outside it, so there is only ever one copy of anything to keep in order. Supported
# extensions are .jpg .jpeg .png .webp .gif — HEIC straight off an iPhone has to be converted
# first, as the decoder cannot read it.
#
# Where generated thumbnails are cached. Safe to delete at any time; it is rebuilt on demand.
# Defaults to <local application data>/pictura-vita/thumbnails.
# IMAGE_CACHE_PATH='/path/to/cache'


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Episode narratives (all optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A narrative is the long-form Markdown account of an episode. As with images the store
# holds only a file name, and the text lives on disk:
#
#   <narrative root>/<timeline id>/<file name>.md
#
# By default the narrative root is "narratives" beside DATA_FILE_PATH, so the JSON, the
# pictures and the prose stay one portable directory. Set this only if they have to live
# apart; two independently configured paths drift, and then "portable" stops being true.
# NARRATIVE_ROOT_PATH='/path/to/narratives'
#
# A missing narrative root is not an error. An episode without a narrative is an ordinary
# episode, and one whose file is absent simply offers nothing to read.
#
# Only .md is served. Files written by the app are named after the episode's title
# ("moving-to-kalamazoo.md"); files you drop in by hand work just as well, and can be picked
# from the list in the episode dialog.
